window.docContents[17].resolve({"/Decidability___-Finiteness-and-Degrees/#section-set-builder-notation":{"contents":"Both Set and Finset can be written with the same set-builder notation\n{x | p x}, but the two behave differently.\n\nFor a Set α the set-builder {x | p x} stands for Set.ofPred (fun x => p x), the\nset of all x : α satisfying p. No finiteness or decidability is required,\nbecause a Set α is just a predicate α → Prop wrapped in a one-field structure,\nand membership unwraps it again: x ∈ Set.ofPred p is definitionally p x.\n\nexample : {x : ℕ | x < 3} = Set.ofPred (fun x => x < 3) := rfl\n\n\nexample {α : Type*} (p : α → Prop) (x : α) : x ∈ Set.ofPred p ↔ p x := Iff.rfl\n\n\nThe neighbourhoods and edge set of a graph are sets of exactly this form. For\ninstance G.neighborSet v is definitionally the set-builder {w | G.Adj v w}.\n\nexample {V : Type*} (G : SimpleGraph V) (v : V) :\n    G.neighborSet v = {w | G.Adj v w} := rfl\n\n\nA Finset α, by contrast, must actually be finite. The fundamental way to build one\nusing a predicate is the form {x ∈ s | p x}, which filters an existing\ns : Finset α, retaining only those elements that satisfy p. This is notation for\nFinset.filter, and because Lean has to test p on each element it needs a\nDecidablePred p instance.\n\nexample (s : Finset ℕ) : {x ∈ s | x < 3} = s.filter (fun x => x < 3) := rfl\n#eval ({1, 2, 4, 5} : Finset ℕ).filter (fun x => x > 3) -- {4, 5}\n\n\nThe plain set-builder {x | p x} can also produce a Finset: it is the special case\nof {x ∈ s | p x} in which s is Finset.univ, the Finset of all elements of\nthe ambient type. This only makes sense when there is a [Fintype α] instance.\n(Fintype instances and Finset.univ are introduced properly in\nFintypes.)\n\nLeft to its own devices {x | p x} defaults to producing a Set α, but when the\nexpected type is Finset α and Lean can find a Fintype α instance (so that\nFinset.univ exists) together with a DecidablePred p, the very same notation\nelaborates to the Finset α {x ∈ Finset.univ | p x}, i.e. Finset.univ.filter p.\n\nexample {α : Type*} [Fintype α] (p : α → Prop) [DecidablePred p] :\n    ({x | p x} : Finset α) = Finset.univ.filter (fun x => p x) := rfl\n\n\n#eval ({x | x < 3} : Finset (Fin 5))               -- {0, 1, 2}\n#eval {x ∈ ({0, 1, 2, 3, 4} : Finset ℕ) | x < 3}   -- {0, 1, 2}\n\n\nFinally the {x ∈ s | p x} notation also works for Set α where it abbreviates\nSet.ofPred (fun x => x ∈ s ∧ p x). This does not require decidability.\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Decidability, Finiteness and Degrees\u0009Finite Types","header":"4.2.2. Set-Builder Notation","id":"/Decidability___-Finiteness-and-Degrees/#section-set-builder-notation"},"/Distance/#section-exercises-distance":{"contents":"1. Show that two vertices with a common neighbour are at distance at most 2.\n\nend SimpleGraph\nend\nnamespace SimpleGraph\n\n\nvariable {V : Type*} {G : SimpleGraph V}\nexample {u v w : V} (huw : G.Adj u w) (hwv : G.Adj w v) : G.edist u v ≤ 2 := by\n  sorry\n\n\n2. Show that in a triangle-free graph two distinct vertices with a common neighbour\nare at distance exactly 2. Hint: edist_eq_two_iff.\n\nexample (h3 : G.CliqueFree 3) {u v w : V} (huw : G.Adj u w) (hwv : G.Adj w v)\n    (huv : u ≠ v) : G.edist u v = 2 := by\n  sorry\n\n\n3. Deduce from Hom.edist_le that adding edges cannot increase distances (this is\nMathlib's edist_anti), and that the distance between two vertices of an induced\nsubgraph is at least their distance in G. Hint: Hom.ofLE and\nEmbedding.induce.\n\nexample {H : SimpleGraph V} (h : G ≤ H) (u v : V) : H.edist u v ≤ G.edist u v := by\n  sorry\n\n\nexample (s : Set V) (u v : s) : G.edist u v ≤ (G.induce s).edist u v := by\n  sorry\n\n\n4. Show that dist does not satisfy the triangle inequality in general: find a\ngraph G on Fin 3 and vertices u, v, w with\nG.dist u v + G.dist v w < G.dist u w. Hint: an edge plus an isolated vertex, and\ndist_eq_zero_iff_eq_or_not_reachable.\n\nexample : ∃ (G : SimpleGraph (Fin 3)) (u v w : Fin 3),\n    G.dist u v + G.dist v w < G.dist u w := by\n  sorry\n\n\n5. Use edist_eq_hammingDist to show that the Hamming distance (between vertices of\nthe hypercube) satisfies the triangle inequality.\n\nHint: rewrite the triangle inequality for edist in Q[n] using\nedist_eq_hammingDist, and cast the result back to ℕ.\n\nexample {n : ℕ} (u v w : HypercubeVert n) :\n    u.hammingDist w ≤ u.hammingDist v + v.hammingDist w := by\n  sorry\n\n\n6. Show that every vertex of the cycle graph C[n + 3] has eccentricity\n(n + 3) / 2.\n\nHint: in a vertex-transitive graph all the eccentricities are equal\n(IsVertexTransitive.eccent_eq), and the diameter is their supremum (ediam).\nSince all the eccentricities equal C[n + 3].eccent v, so does their supremum, the\ndiameter, which we computed in ediam_cycleGraph.\n\nexample (n : ℕ) (v : Fin (n + 3)) : C[n + 3].eccent v = ((n + 3) / 2 : ℕ) := by\n  sorry\n\n\n7. Show that every vertex of the hypercube Q[n] has eccentricity n.\n\nexample {n : ℕ} (v : HypercubeVert n) : Q[n].eccent v = n := by\n  sorry\n\n\n8. Show that the complete bipartite graph K[s + 2, t + 1] has diameter 2.\n\nexample (s t : ℕ) : K[s + 2, t + 1].ediam = 2 := by\n  sorry\n\n\n9. Show that if G has diameter at least 3 then its complement has diameter at\nmost 3.\n\nHint: take two vertices u and v at distance more than 2 in G\n(two_lt_edist_iff), and show that every vertex is adjacent to u or to v in\nGᶜ, or equal to one of them.\n\nexample (h : 3 ≤ G.ediam) : Gᶜ.ediam ≤ 3 := by\n  sorry\n\n\n10. Show that if v has a neighbour in G then in the Mycielskian mμ(G) the two\ncopies vˡ and vʳ of v are at distance exactly 2. Hint: edist_eq_two_iff.\n\nexample {u v : V} (h : G.Adj u v) : mμ(G).edist vˡ vʳ = 2 := by\n  sorry\n\n\n11. Show that the Grötzsch graph mμ(C[5]) has girth 4. Hint: use\negirth_mycielskian_eq_four.\n\nexample : grotzschGraph.egirth = 4 := by\n  sorry\n\n\n12. Use the Moore bound to show that a 3-regular graph of diameter at most 2 has\nat most 10 vertices. (The Petersen graph shows that this is best possible.)\n\nexample [Fintype V] [DecidableRel G.Adj]\n    (h : G.IsRegularOfDegree 3) (hd : G.ediam ≤ 2) : Fintype.card V ≤ 10 := by\n  sorry\n\n\n13. Show that the Moore bound holds with local finiteness as the only finiteness\nassumption: a locally finite graph with maximum degree at most d + 1 and diameter\nat most k is finite, and has at most 1 + (d + 1) * (1 + d + ⋯ + d ^ (k - 1))\nvertices. Hint: finite_of_eccent_le and moore_bound.\n\nexample [G.LocallyFinite] (d k : ℕ)\n    (h_deg : ∀ v, G.degree v ≤ d + 1) (h : G.ediam ≤ k) :\n    Finite V ∧ Nat.card V ≤ 1 + (d + 1) * ∑ i ∈ Finset.range k, d ^ i := by\n  sorry\n\n\n14. Use the cage bound to show that a graph with minimum degree at least 3 and\ngirth at least 5 has at least 10 vertices. (The Petersen graph shows that this\nis best possible.)\n\nexample [Fintype V] [Nonempty V] [DecidableRel G.Adj]\n    (h : ∀ v, 3 ≤ G.degree v) (hg : 5 ≤ G.egirth) : 10 ≤ Fintype.card V := by\n  sorry\n\n\n1. Show that two vertices with a common neighbour are at distance at most 2.example {u v w : V} (huw : G.Adj u w) (hwv : G.Adj w v) : G.edist u v ≤ 2 :=\n  (edist_le (Walk.cons huw (Walk.cons hwv Walk.nil))).trans (by simp)\n2. Show that in a triangle-free graph two distinct vertices with a common neighbour\nare at distance exactly 2. Hint: edist_eq_two_iff.The vertices u and v are not adjacent, since otherwise u, v and w would\nform a triangle, and w is a common neighbour.example (h3 : G.CliqueFree 3) {u v w : V} (huw : G.Adj u w) (hwv : G.Adj w v)\n    (huv : u ≠ v) : G.edist u v = 2 := by\n  classical\n  exact edist_eq_two_iff.mpr ⟨huv, fun h => h3 _ (is3Clique_triple_iff.2\n    ⟨h, huw, hwv.symm⟩), w, G.mem_commonNeighbors.2 ⟨huw, hwv.symm⟩⟩\n3. Deduce from Hom.edist_le that adding edges cannot increase distances (this is\nMathlib's edist_anti), and that the distance between two vertices of an induced\nsubgraph is at least their distance in G. Hint: Hom.ofLE and\nEmbedding.induce.Both inequalities apply Hom.edist_le to a homomorphism that is the identity on\nvertices: the inclusion Hom.ofLE h : G →g H, and the embedding\nEmbedding.induce s : G.induce s ↪g G of the induced subgraph.example {H : SimpleGraph V} (h : G ≤ H) (u v : V) : H.edist u v ≤ G.edist u v :=\n  (Hom.ofLE h).edist_le u v\nexample (s : Set V) (u v : s) : G.edist u v ≤ (G.induce s).edist u v :=\n  (Embedding.induce s).toHom.edist_le u v\n4. Show that dist does not satisfy the triangle inequality in general: find a\ngraph G on Fin 3 and vertices u, v, w with\nG.dist u v + G.dist v w < G.dist u w. Hint: an edge plus an isolated vertex, and\ndist_eq_zero_iff_eq_or_not_reachable.Take the edge 0 — 1 and the isolated vertex 2. No walk leaves 2, so 2 is\nreachable only from itself, and the junk value 0 gives\nG.dist 0 2 + G.dist 2 1 = 0 < 1 = G.dist 0 1.example : ∃ (G : SimpleGraph (Fin 3)) (u v w : Fin 3),\n    G.dist u v + G.dist v w < G.dist u w := by\n  let G := fromRel fun a b : Fin 3 => a = 0 ∧ b = 1\n  have h2 (x : Fin 3) (h : G.Reachable 2 x) : x = 2 := by\n    obtain ⟨p⟩ := h\n    cases p with\n    | nil => rfl\n    | @cons _ y _ ha _ =>\n      fin_cases y <;> exact absurd ha (by decide)\n  have h01 : G.dist 0 1 = 1 := dist_eq_one_iff_adj.mpr (by decide)\n  have h02 : G.dist 0 2 = 0 := dist_eq_zero_iff_eq_or_not_reachable.mpr\n    (.inr fun h => absurd (h2 0 h.symm) (by decide))\n  have h21 : G.dist 2 1 = 0 := dist_eq_zero_iff_eq_or_not_reachable.mpr\n    (.inr fun h => absurd (h2 1 h) (by decide))\n  exact ⟨G, 0, 2, 1, by rw [h01, h02, h21]; norm_num⟩\n5. Use edist_eq_hammingDist to show that the Hamming distance (between vertices of\nthe hypercube) satisfies the triangle inequality.Hint: rewrite the triangle inequality for edist in Q[n] using\nedist_eq_hammingDist, and cast the result back to ℕ.example {n : ℕ} (u v w : HypercubeVert n) :\n    u.hammingDist w ≤ u.hammingDist v + v.hammingDist w := by\n  have := Q[n].edist_triangle (u := u) (v := v) (w := w)\n  rw [edist_eq_hammingDist, edist_eq_hammingDist, edist_eq_hammingDist] at this\n  exact_mod_cast this\n6. Show that every vertex of the cycle graph C[n + 3] has eccentricity\n(n + 3) / 2.Hint: in a vertex-transitive graph all the eccentricities are equal\n(IsVertexTransitive.eccent_eq), and the diameter is their supremum (ediam).\nSince all the eccentricities equal C[n + 3].eccent v, so does their supremum, the\ndiameter, which we computed in ediam_cycleGraph.example (n : ℕ) (v : Fin (n + 3)) : C[n + 3].eccent v = ((n + 3) / 2 : ℕ) := by\n  rw [← ediam_cycleGraph n, ediam,\n    iSup_congr fun u => (isVertexTransitive_cycleGraph _).eccent_eq u v, iSup_const]\n7. Show that every vertex of the hypercube Q[n] has eccentricity n.example {n : ℕ} (v : HypercubeVert n) : Q[n].eccent v = n :=\n  le_antisymm (eccent_le_ediam.trans ediam_hypercube.le)\n    (radius_hypercube.ge.trans radius_le_eccent)\n8. Show that the complete bipartite graph K[s + 2, t + 1] has diameter 2.In the complete bipartite graph any two vertices on the same side have a common\nneighbour on the other side, so the diameter is at most 2, while two distinct\nvertices on the same side are not adjacent, so it is at least 2.example (s t : ℕ) : K[s + 2, t + 1].ediam = 2 := by\n  refine le_antisymm (ediam_le_of_edist_le fun u v => not_lt.1 fun h => ?_) ?_\n  · obtain ⟨-, hadj, hcn⟩ := two_lt_edist_iff.mp h\n    rw [Set.eq_empty_iff_forall_notMem] at hcn\n    rcases u with a | a <;> rcases v with b | b\n    · exact hcn (.inr 0) (by simp [mem_commonNeighbors])\n    · simp at hadj\n    · simp at hadj\n    · exact hcn (.inl 0) (by simp [mem_commonNeighbors])\n  · exact (edist_eq_two_iff.mpr ⟨by simp, by simp, .inr 0,\n      by simp [mem_commonNeighbors]⟩).ge.trans\n      (edist_le_ediam (u := .inl 0) (v := .inl 1))\n9. Show that if G has diameter at least 3 then its complement has diameter at\nmost 3.Hint: take two vertices u and v at distance more than 2 in G\n(two_lt_edist_iff), and show that every vertex is adjacent to u or to v in\nGᶜ, or equal to one of them.example (h : 3 ≤ G.ediam) : Gᶜ.ediam ≤ 3 := by\n  obtain ⟨u, v, huv⟩ : ∃ u v, 2 < G.edist u v := by\n    by_contra! hc\n    exact absurd (h.trans (ediam_le_of_edist_le hc)) (by decide)\n  obtain ⟨hne, hnadj, hcn⟩ := two_lt_edist_iff.mp huv\n  -- in Gᶜ, every vertex is within distance 1 of u or v, and u and v are\n  -- within distance 1 of each other\n  have hnear : ∀ x, ∃ w ∈ ({u, v} : Set V), Gᶜ.edist x w ≤ 1 := by\n    intro x\n    simp only [Set.mem_insert_iff, Set.mem_singleton_iff, exists_eq_or_imp,\n      exists_eq_left, edist_le_one_iff_adj_or_eq, compl_adj]\n    by_contra! hx\n    have : x ∈ G.commonNeighbors u v := by\n      simp only [mem_commonNeighbors]\n      exact ⟨(hx.1.1 hx.1.2).symm, (hx.2.1 hx.2.2).symm⟩\n    simp [hcn] at this\n  have huv1 : Gᶜ.edist u v ≤ 1 := edist_le_one_iff_adj_or_eq.mpr (.inl ⟨hne, hnadj⟩)\n  have hpair : ∀ a ∈ ({u, v} : Set V), ∀ b ∈ ({u, v} : Set V), Gᶜ.edist a b ≤ 1 := by\n    rintro a (rfl | rfl) b (rfl | rfl) <;>\n      first | simp | exact huv1 | exact edist_comm.trans_le huv1\n  refine ediam_le_of_edist_le fun x y => ?_\n  obtain ⟨a, ha, hxa⟩ := hnear x\n  obtain ⟨b, hb, hyb⟩ := hnear y\n  calc Gᶜ.edist x y ≤ Gᶜ.edist x a + Gᶜ.edist a b + Gᶜ.edist b y :=\n        Gᶜ.edist_triangle.trans (add_le_add_left Gᶜ.edist_triangle _)\n    _ ≤ 1 + 1 + 1 := by gcongr; exacts [hpair a ha b hb, edist_comm.trans_le hyb]\n    _ = 3 := by norm_num\n10. Show that if v has a neighbour in G then in the Mycielskian mμ(G) the two\ncopies vˡ and vʳ of v are at distance exactly 2. Hint: edist_eq_two_iff.The two copies of v are distinct and not adjacent (v is not adjacent to itself\nin G), and the left copy uˡ of any neighbour u of v is a common neighbour.example {u v : V} (h : G.Adj u v) : mμ(G).edist vˡ vʳ = 2 := by\n  rw [edist_eq_two_iff]\n  refine ⟨by simp, by simp, ⟨uˡ, ?_⟩⟩\n  rw [mem_commonNeighbors]\n  simp [h.symm]\n11. Show that the Grötzsch graph mμ(C[5]) has girth 4. Hint: use\negirth_mycielskian_eq_four.C[5] is triangle-free, and its vertex 0 has the two neighbours 1 and 4, so\negirth_mycielskian_eq_four applies. Both facts are finite checks.example : grotzschGraph.egirth = 4 := by\n  refine egirth_mycielskian_eq_four (by decide) fun h => ?_\n  exact absurd (h 0 1 4 (by decide) (by decide)) (by decide)\n12. Use the Moore bound to show that a 3-regular graph of diameter at most 2 has\nat most 10 vertices. (The Petersen graph shows that this is best possible.)example [Fintype V] [DecidableRel G.Adj]\n    (h : G.IsRegularOfDegree 3) (hd : G.ediam ≤ 2) : Fintype.card V ≤ 10 := by\n  simpa [Finset.sum_range_succ] using moore_bound G 2 2 (fun v => (h v).le) hd\n13. Show that the Moore bound holds with local finiteness as the only finiteness\nassumption: a locally finite graph with maximum degree at most d + 1 and diameter\nat most k is finite, and has at most 1 + (d + 1) * (1 + d + ⋯ + d ^ (k - 1))\nvertices. Hint: finite_of_eccent_le and moore_bound.A graph with no vertices is finite. Otherwise any vertex r has eccentricity at most\nthe diameter, so V is finite by finite_of_eccent_le, and we can apply\nmoore_bound.example [G.LocallyFinite] (d k : ℕ)\n    (h_deg : ∀ v, G.degree v ≤ d + 1) (h : G.ediam ≤ k) :\n    Finite V ∧ Nat.card V ≤ 1 + (d + 1) * ∑ i ∈ Finset.range k, d ^ i := by\n  rcases isEmpty_or_nonempty V with hV | ⟨⟨r⟩⟩\n  · exact ⟨inferInstance, by simp⟩\n  have hfin : Finite V := finite_of_eccent_le (eccent_le_ediam (u := r) |>.trans h)\n  exact ⟨hfin, moore_bound G d k h_deg h⟩\n14. Use the cage bound to show that a graph with minimum degree at least 3 and\ngirth at least 5 has at least 10 vertices. (The Petersen graph shows that this\nis best possible.)example [Fintype V] [Nonempty V] [DecidableRel G.Adj]\n    (h : ∀ v, 3 ≤ G.degree v) (hg : 5 ≤ G.egirth) : 10 ≤ Fintype.card V := by\n  simpa [Finset.sum_range_succ, ENat.card_eq_coe_fintype_card] using\n    cage_bound_odd G 2 2 (fun v => h v) hg\n\n\nend SimpleGraph\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Distance","header":"11.6. Exercises","id":"/Distance/#section-exercises-distance"}});